Dynamic arrays, also known as resizable arrays, are arrays that can automatically adjust their size as needed. Unlike static arrays, which have a fixed size at compile time, dynamic arrays can grow or shrink during runtime. This is typically implemented by allocating a new, larger array and copying the elements from the old array to the new one. Common implementations include ArrayList in Java and vector in C++. Resizing can be an expensive operation, as it involves copying all the elements. To mitigate this, dynamic arrays often allocate more space than is currently needed, using a growth factor (e.g., doubling the size) to reduce the frequency of resizing. Understanding the trade-offs between space usage and resizing overhead is crucial when using dynamic arrays.

```java
ArrayList<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();
list.add(10);
list.add(20);
// The ArrayList automatically resizes as needed
```

Consider the performance implications of frequent resizing when choosing between a dynamic array and a linked list, especially when dealing with a large number of insertions.

A singly linked list is a linear data structure where each element, called a node, contains data and a pointer (or reference) to the next node in the sequence. The first node is called the head, and the last node's pointer is typically set to null. Singly linked lists allow for efficient insertion and deletion of elements at any position, but accessing an element requires traversing the list from the head, resulting in O(n) time complexity. Common operations include insertion, deletion, searching, and reversing the list. They are memory-efficient compared to arrays, as they only allocate space for the elements that are actually stored.

```c++
struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* next;
};
```

Consider the trade-offs between access time and insertion/deletion time when choosing a linked list versus an array.  Linked lists are particularly useful when the number of elements is unknown or changes frequently.

A doubly linked list is similar to a singly linked list, but each node also contains a pointer to the previous node in the sequence. This allows for bidirectional traversal, making it easier to navigate the list and perform operations like deletion and insertion. However, doubly linked lists require more memory than singly linked lists, as each node needs to store two pointers. Common operations include insertion, deletion, searching, and reversing the list. Deletion is more efficient in doubly linked lists compared to singly linked lists, as you can directly access the previous node without traversing from the head.

```java
class Node {
    int data;
    Node prev;
    Node next;
}
```

Consider the memory overhead versus the improved efficiency of certain operations when choosing a doubly linked list versus a singly linked list.  Doubly linked lists are useful when frequent backward traversal is required.

A queue is a linear data structure that follows the First-In, First-Out (FIFO) principle. Elements are added to the rear (enqueue) and removed from the front (dequeue). Common operations include enqueue, dequeue, peek (accessing the front element), and isEmpty (checking if the queue is empty). Queues are commonly used for task scheduling, print queues, and breadth-first search algorithms. Implementing a queue can be done using arrays or linked lists. Circular queues are often used to avoid wasting space when using arrays. Queues are essential for managing resources and processing tasks in order.

```c++
#include <queue>
std::queue<int> q;
q.push(10); // Enqueue
q.push(20);
std::cout << q.front() << std::endl; // Peek: prints 10
q.pop(); // Dequeue
```

Consider the FIFO nature of queues when choosing them for specific problems.  They are particularly well-suited for scenarios where elements need to be processed in the order they were received.
